            "text": "Makes a tensor filled with zeros, in the shape you give it.\n\nOn its own it is the dullest tensor there is, and that is its use: a blank to start from. Accumulate into it, add values into part of it, or hold a correctly shaped place in a chain until real data arrives.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.zeros <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ... <dtype> <device>\nOne whole number per dimension: t.zeros 8 8 is an 8 by 8 tensor,\nt.zeros 16 a row of 16. With no numbers you get a 1-element tensor.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.zeros 8 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, to send the tensor. Nothing else makes the node send; each click sends a new tensor.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe dimensions, typed as numbers separated by spaces or commas - '8 8', '[4, 16]' or '2 3 4' for 3-D. A list or tensor of numbers sent into the inlet works too (a message '4 16', or a shape from another node), and so does a single number (16 is a row of 16). Unlike np.zeros, the number of dimensions can change here without retyping the node. A new shape does not send; the next click uses it.\n\ndtype (menu):\nThe number type. float32 is the default and what most torch operations want. The menu also offers float16, int64, uint8, bool, complex64 and complex128; 'float' is the same as float32, and each name appears a second time with a 'torch.' prefix, which means the same thing. There is no 64-bit float here.\n\ndevice (menu):\nWhere the tensor is made: cpu (the default), and mps or cuda when the machine has them. Tensors on different devices cannot be combined, so keep a chain on one device.\n\nrequires_grad (checkbox):\nRecords operations for automatic differentiation. Leave it off unless you are training something. Only float and complex tensors can carry it: ticked with an integer or bool dtype, the node raises an error and sends nothing.\n\nA dtype or device name can also be typed as an argument, after the numbers - for example t.zeros 8 8 int64.\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor of zeros:\nThe tensor. A bool dtype gives all false.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe load_bang clicks t.zeros once when the page opens. The heat_map is the tensor - every cell 0, one flat colour, each printing its 0. t.info reports its shape, dtype and device: [8, 8], torch.float32, cpu. Click the message '4 8': 't bang list' sends the list into the shape inlet and THEN clicks the node, so the picture becomes 4 rows of 8 and t.info follows; '8 8' puts it back. Typing a shape into the node and clicking its button does the same. Choose int64 as dtype and click again to see t.info change.\n\nRELATED:\nt.ones is the same with every value 1, t.full with any value you pick. t.zeros_like takes its shape from another tensor. t.eye puts ones on the diagonal of a square of zeros. np.zeros is the NumPy version."
        },
